 "title": "The smallest knot space that counts writes the count in binary",
 "subject": "Which counts of held points a knot space with the not-all-equal law can hold",
 "conclusion": "The eight-point torus that computes the parity of three held points also reads their majority at another point, so it holds their count as two binary digits, a full adder, at the least size at which any knot space counts (exact). Such spaces hold a count mod m only for even m and an odd number of held points, so counting in threes needs a value fixed from outside (proved).",
